    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media from left to right agree that the share of asylum seekers receiving state benefits has dropped markedly, indicating a shift in the composition of migration flows. They uniformly criticize the emerging narrative that frames deportations as a militarised “remigration” programme, calling it legally questionable and socially polarising. At the same time, all outlets stress the urgency of tighter border management, repeatedly reporting on incidents in Ceuta, the Mediterranean and the prospect of using security assets for returns.",
      "controversial_points": "The core conflict pits humanitarian asylum principles against a security‑driven “remigration” narrative. Left‑leaning outlets frame deportations as human rights violations, while right‑wing media portray them as necessary border protection. Conservatives focus on fiscal strain of integration, emphasizing cost‑benefit calculations over cultural arguments. The debate also splits on EU solidarity versus national sovereignty, with parties contesting shared responsibility for external border control."
